  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "wYnPR2f1yyWTfVksyGYcfG/pcHQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:50.083Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`IDocumentIndexer` (`src/storage/kysely/document-indexer.ts`) is the read model behind every relationship question. It indexes exactly two operations \u2014 `ADD_RELATIONSHIP` creates an edge, `REMOVE_RELATIONSHIP` removes one \u2014 and exposes four queries:\n\n- **`getOutgoing()`** \u2014 children and other targets of a document;\n- **`getIncoming()`** \u2014 parents and other sources;\n- **`findPath()`** \u2014 path finding by breadth-first search;\n- **`findAncestors()`** \u2014 the full ancestor graph.\n\nThe last two are the ones worth remembering, because they are not derivable from a document's own state at any reasonable cost: a path or an ancestor set spans documents, and each document knows nothing about its parents. The relationship table is indexed on both `sourceId` and `targetId`, which is what makes the graph genuinely bidirectional rather than a parent pointer plus a scan.\n\n`getOutgoing` / `getIncoming` are paged (`{ cursor, limit }`), so enumeration of a large container is DB-native rather than a state read.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 Components \u203a DocumentIndexer\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:18:50.083Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:50.083Z"
    }
  },
